maya: Heads-up — hit a flaky client case in the new SSE streaming SDK. Large bursts cause split JSON objects across chunks and our parser occasionally blocks when the app is under load. Anyone seen this pattern?

jon: yup seen that before. Is this the Node fetch path or browser?

maya: Node (18 fetch) but repros in browser too. Using a small streaming parser that accumulates bytes until it sees a matching `}` then tries JSON.parse. Works 99% but fails on multibyte split and when backlog grows.

sam: can you paste a minimal chunk trace?

maya: here's a trimmed log showing chunk boundaries and lengths:

```
CHUNK 1 (len=128): "{\"id\":1,\"text\":\"Hello \"
CHUNK 2 (len=12): "world", \"meta\":{
CHUNK 3 (len=64): "\"ts\":168...}}\n"
```

maya: our parser sees CHUNK1, tries to parse -> incomplete, buffers. CHUNK2+3 arrive quickly; but under CPU pressure the consumer cannot process fast enough and the internal buffer grows until we hit OOM in a stress test.

liam: two things: 1) multibyte utf-8 boundaries can split inside a codepoint; ensure you use TextDecoder with `stream: true`. 2) backpressure — if downstream is slow, don't keep appending unboundedly; apply a simple queue size cap and pause the source if possible.

anya: for Node stream reader pattern we do something like:

```
const reader = body.getReader();
const dec = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while (true) {
  const { done, value } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  buf += dec.decode(value, { stream: true });
  // extract full objects/newlines
  let idx;
  while ((idx = buf.indexOf('\n')) !== -1) {
    const line = buf.slice(0, idx);
    buf = buf.slice(idx + 1);
    handleLine(line);
  }
}
// final flush
if (buf.trim()) handleLine(buf);
```

anya: switching to "+newline+delimited JSON" (NDJSON) from the server side helps a lot — no need to match braces.

jon: +1 NDJSON. If server can't change, consider framing (length-prefixed) or using a small state machine to track braces and string escapes instead of naive indexOf. Brace counting needs to handle `\"` escapes though.

sam: re backpressure: Node's fetch ReadableStream doesn't expose pause() easily. We solved this with a bounded async queue — when queue.length > N we call `controller.close()`? careful with that. Another pattern is to `await drain()` from the consumer before reading more.

maya: here's the simplified bounded queue we tried (pseudo):

```
const q = [];
const MAX = 1000;
async function producer(reader) {
  while (true) {
    const {done, value} = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    q.push(value);
    if (q.length > MAX) {
      // backpressure: wait until consumer drains to < MAX/2
      await waitForDrain();
    }
  }
}
```

maya: problem is waitForDrain relies on promise signaling and in heavy GC it still falls behind and memory grows.

liam: can you try smaller MAX first and instrument queue sizes/timings? Also run with `--trace-gc` to see if GC pause correlates.

anya: another trick: decode each chunk immediately to string and push only parsed objects into queue. That reduces retained raw Buffer memory. Use `dec.decode(value, {stream:true})` then extract messages so you never store whole Buffer arrays.

jon: one more: if partial JSON is only due to arrays/objects, a tiny stateful parser that tracks bracket depth and whether inside string is robust and cheap. Example: on every char update depth, toggle inString on `\"` except when preceded by `\\`. When depth==0 emit.

maya: good call — I'll try bracket-depth + TextDecoder streaming, plus NDJSON fallback if we can get server change. Also add metrics for queue length and time-in-queue.

sam: ping me when you have the repro script, I can run a stress harness. Would be great to have a small reproducer in `devtools/stream-repro` so infra can run on CI.

maya: plan: 1) implement decoder+stateful parser 2) cap queue to 200 parsed msgs and `await drain()` rather than raw buffers 3) add `sdk.streaming.queue_size` metric and GC traces. I'll open a PR and link the reproducer.

jon: sounds good. also @infra-bot can we carve out a small GPU node for load runs? CPU-bound but want isolation.

